authority]], :meets {[:hygiene/observe-the-authority :process/spec-drift-by-displacement] :hygiene/observe-the-authority, [:orchestration/pattern-warranted-choice-point :sidecar/explicit-promotion-to-facts] :sidecar/explicit-promotion-to-facts}, :missing-meets [], :precedence-violations []}, :stopped-is-not-success true, :target "M-autoclock-in", :family-searched 2, :budget-used 1}}, :at "2026-09-26T01:18:51.177583439Z"}, :reading "Same interpretation as my earlier answer for this token; the pattern bytes are unchanged. The pattern says to 'gate successful responses on durable persistence ... verify durability ... and reject success when persistence fails'. Its reasons are that 'acknowledging success before persistence creates silent loss modes (accepted-but-not-stored)' and that 'a proof-path is only real if it can be reconstructed later'. The acceptance criterion's clauses map onto it as follows. 'Read both cases back through a newly constructed real durable backend client' is the reconstruct-later test: the decisions have to survive outside the process that wrote them, not only in its cache. 'If recording fails, assert it is surfaced as an accounting failure, not reported as a successful recorded decision' is the pattern's 'reject success when persistence fails'. It closes the accepted-but-not-stored case, where an agent turn runs even though its clock decision silently failed to persist. 'Every accepted turn must have a persisted decision' is the gate itself: admission waits for durability. 'Keep absence and storage failure distinct' stops a lost write from being recorded as a legitimate :unclocked. The matching checks are complete-session-decisions-roundtrip-real-backend, persistence-failure-prevents-agent-execution and swallowed-callback-accounting-failure-fails-the-session.", :response-id "response-c33ca1e6c97b1e2a", :answered-by {:seat "claude-5", :job-id "invoke-1790385510981-24593-3b10fbbb"}}, :process/spec-silence-pair {:request-id "request-6308ab6cd962e3