 its refusal and leaves the clock where it was', since that refusal is itself recorded as a decision (kept-because). The pattern's T2a (drift goes where the checker has no jurisdiction) points at the Consequence paragraph: the Emacs buffer is outside the resolver's jurisdiction and still applies the old floor-only rule to operator input.", :response-id "response-f7d6b56cb137b171", :answered-by {:seat "claude-5", :job-id "invoke-1790385174034-24587-deaf9fa2"}}, :hygiene/observe-the-authority {:request-id "request-69bbac2e9ce5cf1d", :validator {:ns "futon2.aif.want-interpretation", :source-sha256 "8cfadab5696338e1c258dd64f4c21bf2c2206614283d428bd8bfb2a5868cbaa4"}, :source {:path "futon3/library/hygiene/observe-the-authority.flexiarg", :sha256 "f43c2229b97e9162b4b62af0093f5d15854423b1fc810cd0da4ccdc5ed568bef"}, :kind :machine-requested, :scope-limit "The pattern's own settings (buffer cleaning, repository hygiene, test-registry environments) do not carry over, and neither does its instruction to 'emit raw signals'. What carries over is the general move of reading the authority instead of trusting the stand-in. The ERT test checks that the buffer adopts the returned decision. It does not re-check the resolver, which clock-decision-test covers. The pattern also does not choose which side is the authority; the mission's amendment makes that choice.", :want :exit/h203ce9577ebe, :by "claude-5", :validated {:checks [:canonical-id :library-source-sha :produces-want :guard-tokens-known :owner-constraints :constructs-through-it :admitted], :candidate {:precedence [:orchestration/pattern-warranted-choice-point :sidecar/explicit-promotion-to-facts :process/spec-drift-by-displacement :hygiene/observe-the-authority :sidecar/proposal-ledger-for-fuzzy-output], :construction-receipt {:locator-coverage :token-set-not-supplied, :unknown-read-as-not-established [], :g-of-best {:value 22.99316913014941, :universe [:exit/h0c55648e57e5 :exit/h0f3acdf222d7 :exit/h1dae607747ce :exit/h203ce957